ATLAS CLOUD — PHASE 2A.2
SAAS CONTROL-PLANE & MULTI-TENANT FOUNDATION
CURRENT STATUS

Phase 1 is complete.

Phase 2A.1 security remediation is complete.

The following security work has already been implemented:

Removed the admin123 plaintext login backdoor
Removed simpleHashCompare
Added bcrypt password verification
Added timing-attack protection
Added preliminary login rate limiting
Added HMAC-SHA256 signed admin sessions
Hardened admin session cookies
Removed hardcoded admin credentials from the UI
Added server-side environment variable documentation
Migrated existing administrative database operations toward server-side credentials

DO NOT redo this work unless inspection reveals an actual defect.

CRITICAL CONSTRAINT

The owner is NOT creating the Supabase project yet.

Therefore:

DO NOT:

create a Supabase project
connect to Supabase
request Supabase credentials
execute remote migrations
deploy Edge Functions
require Supabase for npm run build
implement live customer authentication
implement Stripe
implement DigitalOcean
implement Coolify

Everything in this phase must be prepared locally for future activation.

1. OBJECTIVE

Complete the missing SaaS foundation for Atlas Cloud.

Atlas is becoming a multi-tenant cloud control plane supporting two primary customer journeys:

CLOUD CUSTOMER

Customer

→ Atlas Organization

→ Project

→ Application

→ Environment

→ Infrastructure

→ Deployment

ATLAS PRODUCT CUSTOMER

Customer

→ Atlas Organization

→ Atlas Product

→ Product Instance

→ Project

→ Environment

→ Infrastructure

→ Deployment

The purpose of Phase 2A.2 is to establish the domain model, SQL migrations, tenant boundaries, RLS design, TypeScript models, authorization architecture, entitlement architecture, provisioning model and dashboard foundation required for these workflows.

No actual cloud infrastructure should be created.

2. INSPECT FIRST

Before modifying anything, inspect the current repository after Phase 2A.1.

Specifically identify:

existing migrations
existing Supabase utilities
existing auth utilities
existing admin architecture
existing dashboard routes
config/plans.ts
config/products.ts
existing TypeScript types
middleware
API routes
environment validation
existing tests
current package scripts

Preserve Phase 2A.1 security improvements.

Produce a concise inspection summary before implementation.

3. TENANT MODEL

Atlas must be multi-tenant.

The primary tenant boundary is:

Organization

A user may eventually belong to multiple organizations.

Do NOT design tenant ownership around user_id alone.

Create local migrations for:

organizations

Suggested fields:

id
name
slug
status
created_by
created_at
updated_at

Statuses:

active
suspended
closed

Use appropriate UUIDs, indexes and constraints.

4. ORGANIZATION MEMBERS

Create:

organization_members

Suggested fields:

id
organization_id
user_id
role
status
created_at
updated_at

Initial organization roles:

owner
admin
developer
billing
viewer

Statuses:

active
invited
suspended

Do not scatter arbitrary role strings throughout the application.

Centralize roles/types.

5. PLATFORM ROLES

Keep Atlas internal roles separate from customer roles.

Platform roles may eventually include:

platform_admin
operations
support

Organization roles are:

owner
admin
developer
billing
viewer

An organization owner MUST NOT automatically become an Atlas platform administrator.

Design these as separate authorization domains.

