22. RLS — PREPARE NOW

Write Supabase/PostgreSQL RLS policies locally.

Do not deploy them.

Core tenant rule:

A user can access a tenant-owned resource only when that authenticated user has an active membership in the organization owning that resource and the user's role permits the requested operation.

RLS should cover appropriate tables including:

organizations
organization_members
projects
applications
environments
product_instances
subscriptions
entitlements
infrastructure_resources
provisioning_jobs
deployments
domains
database_resources
audit_logs

Use conservative policies for:

subscriptions
entitlements
infrastructure
provisioning
audit logs
security events

Do not rely upon an organization_id merely because the browser supplied it.

23. SERVICE ROLE BOUNDARY

Review the Phase 2A.1 change that moved existing routes to:

SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY

Do NOT blindly expand this pattern.

Document and enforce the architectural rule:

Normal customer operations

Authenticated user

→ Supabase authenticated context

→ RLS

→ tenant resource

Trusted Atlas system operations

Trusted server process

→ service role

→ explicitly authorized internal operation

Future examples of legitimate service-role usage may include:

Stripe webhook processing
provisioning workers
infrastructure synchronization
controlled administrative jobs

The service role must never become the standard customer data-access mechanism.

Do not expose it to browser code.

24. CONTACT ROUTE SECURITY REVIEW

Review:

/api/contact

It currently uses service-role access following Phase 2A.1.

Determine whether the route genuinely requires service-role privileges.

Do not simply change it for stylistic reasons.

Document:

why the service role is necessary, OR
a safer future approach

Ensure:

strict input validation
bounded input sizes
rate limiting strategy
no arbitrary table access
explicit insert fields
no returned sensitive database content
25. AUTHORIZATION LAYER

Create centralized authorization abstractions.

Conceptually:

requireUser()

requireOrganizationMember()

requireOrganizationRole()

canAccessProject()

canManageApplication()

canManageBilling()

Exact names should follow project conventions.

Do not distribute authorization logic randomly throughout React components.

26. PURE AUTHORIZATION MODEL

Because Supabase does not exist yet, implement pure TypeScript authorization rules that can be unit tested without a database.

For example:

Input:

user

membership

requested action

resource organization

Output:

allow / deny

This allows tenant and role logic to be tested now.

Database RLS will later provide an additional enforcement layer.

27. TENANT ISOLATION TESTS

Create tests demonstrating:

Cross-tenant isolation

User A belongs to Organization A.

User B belongs to Organization B.

Verify:

User A cannot access Organization B.

User A cannot access Project B.

User A cannot access Application B.

User A cannot access Product Instance B.

User A cannot access Environment B.

User A cannot access Infrastructure Resource B.

User A cannot access Provisioning Job B.

Role isolation

Verify:

Viewer cannot perform owner operations.

Billing role does not automatically receive infrastructure administration.

Developer does not automatically receive billing administration.

Organization owner does not become Atlas platform admin.

Unauthenticated access

Unauthenticated identities cannot access tenant resources.

These are authorization unit tests.

Do NOT report these as live Supabase RLS tests.

28. RLS TEST PLAN

Create a future database test/checklist for when Supabase is activated.

It should test:

SELECT

INSERT

UPDATE

DELETE

between:

User A / Organization A

and

User B / Organization B.

Include attempts to manipulate organization_id manually.

These operations must fail across tenant boundaries.

Clearly label this:

NOT EXECUTED — REQUIRES LIVE SUPABASE

